            <div><h3 class="member-name">Mohammad</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science · 1st semester</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="member-body">
            <div class="member-field"><p class="field-label">Into</p><ul class="interest-list"><li>Full-stack development</li><li>Gaming</li></ul></div>
            <div class="member-field"><p class="field-label">A little known fact</p><p>I’ll patiently sit through a 40-hour story-driven game with zero skips, but waiting 30 seconds for a build pipeline to finish tests my entire sanity.</p></div>
            <div class="member-field"><p class="field-label">AI in engineering today</p><p>AI is unmatched for boilerplate and syntax lookups, but it frequently hallucinates subtle logical flaws that make debugging twice as tedious. It speeds up typing without genuinely understanding architecture or context. Right now, it works best as an eager junior developer whose every commit needs thorough code review.</p></div>
            <div class="member-field"><p class="field-label">Looking 5–10 years ahead</p><p>The core job will shift from typing code to specifying requirements, edge cases, and architectural constraints. Autonomous agents may handle boilerplate; engineering value will center on verification, fault tolerance, and knowing what to build.</p></div>
            <div class="member-field"><p class="field-label">Hot take</p><p class="hot-take">“AI doesn’t make bad developers good; it just helps them generate catastrophic technical debt at record speed.”</p></div>
