    <div class="grid">
      <article class="person">
        <div class="person-head"><img class="portrait" src="{{image:Mohammad.png}}" alt="Mohammad"><div><h3 class="person-name">Mohammad</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science · 1st semester</p></div></div>
        <div class="details">
          <div class="field"><p class="label">Interests</p><ul class="interests"><li>Full-stack development</li><li>Gaming</li></ul></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">Fun fact</p><p>I’ll patiently sit through a 40-hour story-driven game with zero skips, but waiting 30 seconds for a build pipeline to finish tests my entire sanity.</p></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">AI in software engineering today</p><p>AI is unmatched for boilerplate and syntax lookups, but frequently hallucinates subtle logical flaws that make debugging twice as tedious. It speeds up typing without genuinely understanding architecture or context. It works best as an eager junior developer whose every commit needs thorough code review.</p></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">5–10 years ahead</p><p>The core job will shift from typing code to specifying requirements, edge cases, and architectural constraints. Autonomous agents may handle boilerplate; engineering value will center on verification, fault tolerance, and knowing what to build.</p></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">Hot take</p><p class="quote">“AI doesn’t make bad developers good; it just helps them generate catastrophic technical debt at record speed.”</p></div>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="person">
        <div class="person-head"><img class="portrait" src="{{image:Nithya.png}}" alt="Nithya"><div><h3 class="person-name">Nithya</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science · 7th semester, thesis stage</p></div></div>
        <div class="details">
          <div class="field"><p class="label">Interests</p><ul class="interests"><li>Distributed systems</li><li>Cloud architecture</li><li>Badminton</li><li>Board games</li></ul></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">Fun fact</p><p>I have rewritten my thesis abstract more times than I have recompiled my actual code.</p></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">AI in software engineering today</p><p>AI assistants multiply productivity for well-understood domains and boilerplate, but struggle with complex legacy codebases. They can be confidently wrong about synchronization and concurrency. AI is a fast copilot, but you cannot let it navigate without senior oversight.</p></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">5–10 years ahead</p><p>Engineering will center on systems integration, formal verification, and distributed consensus. Junior roles may evolve into system orchestration, where understanding failure modes and domain invariants matters more than memorizing library APIs.</p></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">Hot take</p><p class="quote">“AI autocomplete hasn't eliminated bugs; it has just made them compile much faster.”</p></div>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="person">
        <div class="person-head"><img class="portrait" src="{{image:Rafee.png}}" alt="Rafee"><div><h3 class="person-name">Rafee</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science · 1st semester</p></div></div>
        <div class="details">
          <div class="field"><p class="label">Interests</p><ul class="interests"><li>Frontend UI/UX</li><li>React / Next.js</li><li>Table tennis</li><li>Photography</li></ul></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">Fun fact</p><p>I judge coffee shops entirely by their Wi-Fi stability and latency to GitHub.</p></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">AI in software engineering today</p><p>AI is an unbelievable interactive tutor that demystifies obscure compiler errors in seconds. Relying on it too early creates an illusion of competence: you may understand the output without understanding the mechanics beneath it.</p></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">5–10 years ahead</p><p>Natural language and intent-driven wireframes may compile into accessible, responsive interfaces. Frontend engineers can focus on user empathy, accessibility, and high-performance client-side state instead of CSS layout fighting.</p></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">Hot take</p><p class="quote">“If you can't debug code without an LLM, you are the copilot, not the agent.”</p></div>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="person">
        <div class="person-head"><img class="portrait" src="{{image:Raghvendra.png}}" alt="Raghvendra"><div><h3 class="person-name">Raghvendra</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science · 3rd semester</p></div></div>
        <div class="details">
          <div class="field"><p class="label">Interests</p><ul class="interests"><li>Machine learning infrastructure</li><li>Data engineering</li><li>Hiking</li><li>Chess</li></ul></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">Fun fact</p><p>I will spend 4 hours automating a manual 5-minute task, and call it an investment.</p></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">AI in software engineering today</p><p>LLMs are incredible for rapid prototyping and translating business logic into SQL or boilerplate endpoints, but lack deep context and can suggest deprecated methods. Their utility is reducing initial friction, not making core architectural choices.</p></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">5–10 years ahead</p><p>Developers may work with multi-agent pipelines that build, test, and benchmark microservices. Humans will define objectives, enforce privacy boundaries, and resolve conflicting business constraints.</p></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">Hot take</p><p class="quote">“90% of current ‘AI agent’ workflows are just fragile <code>while</code> loops wrapped around regex parsers.”</p></div>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="person">
        <div class="person-head"><img class="portrait" src="{{image:Sayemah.png}}" alt="Sayemah"><div><h3 class="person-name">Sayemah</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science · 1st semester</p></div></div>
        <div class="details">
          <div class="field"><p class="label">Interests</p><ul class="interests"><li>Calligraphy</li><li>Culinary arts &amp; cooking</li><li>Traveling</li><li>Human-Computer Interaction</li></ul></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">Fun fact</p><p>I can master a complex traditional recipe on the first try, but one unmatched curly brace will ruin my entire evening.</p></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">AI in software engineering today</p><p>AI brings creativity and swift prototyping, lowering the barrier to novel ideas. Yet software is also about human communication and interface empathy. Models produce functional code that can lack aesthetic intention and elegance.</p></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">5–10 years ahead</p><p>Software design will become more human-centric and multidisciplinary. Engineers will guide agents with design systems and ethical standards, spending creative energy on user delight and seamless interactions.</p></div>
          <div class="field"><p class="label">Hot take</p><p class="quote">“Prompt engineering is not the future of software engineering; thoughtful human design is.”</p></div>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
